WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.291 --> 00:00:02.085
There’s always an issue 

00:00:02.085 --> 00:00:05.046
happening right now in today’s headlines 

00:00:05.046 --> 00:00:06.923
that links back to the histories  

00:00:06.923 --> 00:00:08.967
we work on at the GLC. 

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:11.219
So what I like to say to people is, 

00:00:11.219 --> 00:00:12.595
what are you interested in? 

00:00:13.304 --> 00:00:14.347
What’s the problem 

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:15.765
you want to solve out there? 

00:00:15.765 --> 00:00:17.976
We might know some history 

00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:19.227
that can help you get there.

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:25.984
I’m currently wrapped up in a project 

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:27.944
President Salovey asked me 

00:00:27.944 --> 00:00:31.114
to direct and manage and write, 

00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:33.533
and that is the history of 

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:37.620
Yale University with slavery and abolition. 

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.498
Yale, as everyone knows, 

00:00:40.498 --> 00:00:43.209
is about 320 years old now. 

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.879
It’s a very long history, 

00:00:47.172 --> 00:00:50.967
and I have led a team, a small team 

00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:53.511
of both faculty and students 

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.681
and librarians who have been crucial to this, 

00:00:57.265 --> 00:01:01.561
to research Yale’s entanglements 

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.813
with the problem of slavery over time. 

00:01:03.855 --> 00:01:05.440
And we made a decision 

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.108
very early in this project 

00:01:07.108 --> 00:01:11.112
not to write a report per se, 

00:01:11.362 --> 00:01:13.406
which is what most universities have done, 

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:15.784
but to write a real narrative history.

00:01:16.826 --> 00:01:19.788
At the GLC, we have managed and organized 

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.834
a whole array of webinars, panels, events 

00:01:25.835 --> 00:01:28.171
for the Yale Project over the past two years. 

00:01:28.171 --> 00:01:31.925
We’ve done panels with the descendants 

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.344
of some of the earliest black Yale students. 

00:01:34.344 --> 00:01:36.429
We’ve done panels with 

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:38.973
local New Haven community leaders. 

00:01:38.973 --> 00:01:40.850
We did panels with undergraduates 

00:01:41.309 --> 00:01:42.477
to know what they thought. 

00:01:42.477 --> 00:01:47.232
We’ve done panels on the history 

00:01:47.232 --> 00:01:49.692
of eugenics at Yale.

00:01:49.901 --> 00:01:53.863
So by managing that project, it gave us 

00:01:53.863 --> 00:01:55.490
another whole set of issues 

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:58.910
to do all kinds of outreach about, 

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.621
because that’s what we’re here for. 

00:02:02.038 --> 00:02:02.831
We’re a center for the study 

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:04.290
of slavery and abolition. 

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:07.710
But it’s remarkable sometimes 

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:11.047
how infinite it feels when you 

00:02:11.047 --> 00:02:12.423
start thinking about all the issues 

00:02:12.423 --> 00:02:14.759
that connect to us, that connect to 

00:02:14.759 --> 00:02:17.887
the questions at the core of our mission: 

00:02:17.887 --> 00:02:18.930
How can we work on 

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.723
this aspect of social justice? 

00:02:20.723 --> 00:02:21.808
How might we work on 

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:25.103
that aspect of repairing the past 

00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:26.855
to the extent we can? 

00:02:26.855 --> 00:02:28.314
How might we work on 

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:29.607
that policy question 

00:02:29.607 --> 00:02:31.943
by doing more history beneath it? 

00:02:33.403 --> 00:02:35.655
How should universities 

00:02:36.239 --> 00:02:38.158
look at their own past 

00:02:38.741 --> 00:02:40.743
and the past around them 

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:43.246
that they helped create?

